.\" Manpage for exclude.conf used by cloneup.sh.
.TH CLONEUP-EXCLUDE.CONF 5 "30 September 2024" "1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
cloneup-exclude.conf \- configuration file for \fBcloneup\fR(1) that contains files/dirs to be excluded from cloning
.SH SYNOPSIS
/etc/cloneup/exclude.conf, /etc/cloneup.d/exclude.conf, ~/.config/cloneup/exclude.conf, <custom pathname>
.SH DESCRIPTION
The default directory of the \fBexclude.conf\fR configuration file is \fI/etc/cloneup/\fR. The file should never be deleted and if the default entries need to be modified it is best to create a new one under \fI/etc/cloneup.d/\fR and/or \fI~/.config/cloneup/\fR but the same filename has to be used. A file under \fI/etc/cloneup.d/\fR modifies (overrides) the default one and one under \fI~/.config/cloneup/\fR replaces it.

\fBcloneup\fR(1) uses the configuration file under \fI~/.config/cloneup/\fR if not run under its directory (usually \fI/usr/sbin/\fR).

A configuration file can also be provided when executing \fBcloneup\fR(1) with the \fB-e\fR or \fB--exclude\fR command line option. It takes priority over any other file and any valid pathname can be used (not restricted to \fBexclude.conf\fR).
.SH EXAMPLES
.I GENERAL USE

-/home/user/Music/* (exclude all files and directories under /home/user/Music/)

-/home/user/Music/  (exclude directory 'Music' under /home/user/)

-/home/user/Music   (exclude a file or directory named 'Music' under /home/user/)

A leading minus (-) sign specifies an exclude entry. Do not use quotes unless they are part of the filename or dirname in which case they have to be escaped, e.g. \\', \\". A trailing '/' specifies a directory to be excluded. No trailing '/' specifies a directory or file to be excluded.

.I TUPLES

If you want to exclude everything under a specific directory except certain files or directories you can use an exclude/include tuple. For example,

-/home/user/*               (exclude all files and directories under /home/user/)

+/home/user/business/report (include a file or directory named 'report' under /home/user/business/)

+/home/user/business/sales/ (include a directory named 'sales' under /home/user/business/)

A leading plus (+) sign specifies an include entry.

A tuple requires at least two entries and one of them has to be an exclude. If there's no exclude entry for an include entry the include entry is ignored. The exclude entry need not preceed the include entries.

If you want to exclude a pathname only if it's a file, you can create a tuple as follows:

-/home/user/report  (exclude a file or directory named 'report')

+/home/user/report/ (include directory 'report')

Another example of a tuple:

-/home/user/a*      (exclude all 'a*' under '/home/user/')

-/home/user/c*      (exclude all 'c*' under '/home/user/')

+/home/user/[ac]z   (include 'az' and 'cz' under '/home/user/')

.I SPECIAL & ESCAPED CHARACTERS

If a filename or dirname includes spaces, parentheses or escaped characters, e.g. newline, they have to be escaped. For example,

[prompt]$ ls -1

\'/home/user/monthly\'$\'\\n\'\'reports/november\'

\'/home/user/sales/november (sorted)\'

The above filenames contain a newline ($\'\\n\'), a space and parentheses. The correct exclude entries, removing unnecessary single quotes, would be

-/home/user/monthly$\'\\n\'reports/november (escaped character syntax: $'\\<ec>')

-/home/user/sales/november\\ \\(sorted\\)   (space and parentheses escaped)

All escaped characters have to be specified as $'\\<ec>'.

.I VALID ENTRIES

Valid entries are:

.B a)\fR blank lines

.B b)\fR comments, a line starting with '#' is a comment.

.B c)\fR any line with the syntax '-|+<fspec>' where:

A leading sign ('-' or '+') is followed by a mandatory file specification (<fspec>). <fspec> specifies pathnames of files or directories (all metacharacters of the bash shell can be used). <fspec> must have a leading '/' and if it has a trailing '/' a directory is specified else a directory or file is specified.
  
.I examples

-/dir1/dir2/report*  (exclude all files and directories beginning with 'report', under '/dir1/dir2/')

-/dir1/dir2/report*/ (exclude directories beginning with 'report', under '/dir1/dir2/')

.I exclude/include tuple example

-/dir1/dir2/          (exclude 'dir1/dir2' under root directory)

+/dir1/dir2/file1.txt (include 'file1.txt' under '/dir1/dir2/')

+/dir1/dir2/file2.txt (include 'file2.txt' under '/dir1/dir2/')

.I other examples

-/dir1/dir2/????.txt

-/dir1/dir2/foot????.doc

-/dir1/dir2/best.???

-/dir1/dir2/*.pl

-/dir1/dir2/a*.*

-/dir1/dir2/[p-s]*             (files/dirs with first char in 'p-s' range)

-/dir1/dir2/[^p-s]*            (files/dirs with first char NOT in 'p-s' range)

-/dir1/dir2/[^p]*              (files/dirs with first char NOT 'p')

-/dir1/dir2/[1-5]*             (files/dirs with first char in '1-5' range)

-/dir1/dir2/{?????.sh,*st.txt} (files that match '?????.sh' AND '*st.txt')

-/dir1/dir2/*{.jpg,.mp3}       (files that have 'jpg' AND 'mp3' extention)

-/dir1/dir2/?(*.jpg|*.mp3)     (0 or 1 file that have 'jpg' OR 'mp3' extention)

-/dir1/dir2/*(*.jpg|*.mp3)     (0 or more files that have 'jpg' OR 'mp3' extention)

-/dir1/dir2/+(*.jpg|*.mp3)     (1 or more files that have 'jpg' OR 'mp3' extention)

-/dir1/dir2/!(*.jpg|*.mp3)     (files that do NOT have 'jpg' OR 'mp3' extention)

.B d)\fR any line with the syntax '-|+/[<path>] [<path>]<name>' where:

A leading sign ('-' or '+') is followed by a '/' and an optional <path> which can have a trailing '/' (all metacharacters of the bash shell can be used in <path>). The next token contains an optional <path> which must not have a leading '/' and a mandatory  <name> which specifies filenames or dirnames. If both <path> and <name> are used they must be separated by a '/'. The following metacharacters can be used in [<path>]<name>: *, ?, [], [^]. <name> can have a trailing '/' which specifies a directory only (no file).

.I invalid examples

The following examples are invalid, as the second token contains some metacharacters that are interpreted as plain characters (valid metacharacters for the second token are *, ?, [], [^]).

-/dir1/di*r2/ +(*.jpg|*.mp3)    , interpreted as plain characters: + ( | )

-/dir1/di*r2  !(*.jpg|*.mp3)    , interpreted as plain characters: ! ( | )

-/dir1/di*r2/ ?(*.jpg|*.mp3)    , interpreted as plain characters: ( | )

-/dir1/di*r2  *(*.jpg|*.mp3)    , interpreted as plain characters: ( | )

-/dir1/di*r2  *{.jpg,.mp3}      , interpreted as plain characters: { , }

-/dir1/di*r2/ {?????.sh,*st.txt}, interpreted as plain characters: { , }

.I examples

-/dir1/dir2/ report* (exclude all files and directories beginning with 'report' that are under '/dir1/dir2/' or any other directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/')

-/dir1/dir2 report*/ (exclude directories beginning with 'report' that are under '/dir1/dir2/' or any other directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/')

-/dir1/dir2/ dir3/report* (exclude all files and directories beginning with 'report' that are under any 'dir3' directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/' or any other directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/')

-/dir1/dir2 dir3/report*/ (exclude directories beginning with 'report' that are under any 'dir3' directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/' or any other directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/')

.I exclude/include tuple example

-/dir1/dir2 dir3/dir4/ (exclude directory 'dir4' that is under any 'dir3' directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/' or any other directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/')

+/dir1/dir2 dir3/dir4/file1.txt (include 'file1.txt' that is under any '/dir3/dir4/' directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/' or any other directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/')

+/dir1/dir2/ dir3/dir4/file2.txt (include 'file2.txt' that is under any '/dir3/dir4/' directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/' or any other directory that is under '/dir1/dir2/')

.I other examples

-/dir1/dir2/  ????.txt

-/dir1/dir2   dir3/dir4/????.txt

-/dir1/di*r2/ foot????.doc

-/dir1/di*r2  dir3/dir4/foot????.doc

-/dir1/dir2/  [p-s]*

-/dir1/dir2/  [p-s]*/

-/dir1/dir2   dir3/dir4/[p-s]*

-/dir1/dir2   dir3/dir4/[p-s]*/

-/dir1/di*r2/ [^p-s]*

-/dir1/di*r2/ [^p-s]*/

-/dir1/di*r2  dir3/dir4/[^p-s]*

-/dir1/di*r2  dir3/dir4/[^p-s]*/

-/dir1/di*r2/ [^p]*

-/dir1/di*r2/ [^p]*/

-/dir1/di*r2  dir3/dir4/[^p]*

-/dir1/di*r2  dir3/dir4/[^p]*/

-/dir1/di*r2/ [1-5]*

-/dir1/di*r2/ [1-5]*/

-/dir1/di*r2  dir3/dir4/[1-5]*

-/dir1/di*r2  dir3/dir4/[1-5]*/

-/dir1/{?????sales,*reports}/ dir2/dir3/[^p-s]*

-/dir1/*{jpg,mp3}             dir2/dir3/[^p-s]*/

-/dir1/?(*jpg|*mp3)/          dir2/dir3/[^1-5]*

-/dir1/*(*jpg|*mp3)           dir2/dir3/[^1-5]*/

-/dir1/+(*jpg|*mp3)/          dir2/dir3/[^p]*

-/dir1/!(*jpg|*mp3)           dir2/dir3/[^p]*/
.SH STANDARDS
The system files excluded from cloning are based on the Filesystem Hierarchy Standard (FHS).
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBcloneup\fR(1)
